## Scope

You have up to 2 tasks to complete:

1. Mitigate every fault found in this cluster and restore the application to a fully healthy state.
2. Diagnose the root cause(s) of the fault(s).

Assume there may be multiple concurrent faults affecting different layers or traffic paths at the same time. While you should aim to reduce e2e time, prioritize reducing time to mitigation — that reduces customer impact. You can submit diagnosis incrementally as you identify root causes, and continue investigating after each submission.

Success criteria:
- A problem is mitigated only when the cluster is genuinely healthy, not merely when obvious crashes stop.
- A mitigation is complete only after you verify pod readiness, service connectivity, representative end-to-end user-facing or caller-facing flows across the system, and that system internals appear healthy rather than partially degraded.
- A diagnosis is complete only when you can state the root cause(s) concisely and defensibly based on live evidence and source/manifests.

Do not make speculative or product-level application changes unrelated to the incident. However, if you confirm that the fault originates from manifests, deployment configuration, scripts, or other source-controlled operational files in this repository, prefer fixing the original file(s) in source first, then apply or redeploy the updated manifests/source so the running cluster reflects that fix, rather than relying only on a one-off live-cluster patch that will be lost on the next deploy.

## Investigation Workflow

Start every run with this preflight workflow.

Operate in an iterative loop:
1. Read the application source code and deployment manifests to contextualize the Kubernetes resources you are seeing. Focus first on the parts of the repo that explain the deployed services, labels/selectors, ports, probes, config references, and cross-service calls.
   As you read, identify representative user-facing or caller-facing flows you can exercise later to prove the application is actually recovered end-to-end.
2. Inspect the live cluster directly with `kubectl` and targeted shell commands. Use multiple views when useful: workloads, events, logs, endpoints, services, ingresses, network policies, PVC/PV state, config objects, and representative requests through the system.
3. Once you identify a symptom, verify it against the live cluster before acting on it. Keep notes and checks lightweight and repeatable, but do not treat script-writing or repo-local tooling as part of the task.
4. Always distinguish between problems explained by the current deployed cluster state and problems explained by source-controlled manifests or deployment config. Only edit source files when you can point to the exact bad setting in the repository. If the fault lives in source-controlled manifests or deployment config, fix the real file in place first, then apply or redeploy the updated manifests/source and verify the running cluster now reflects that source change before you consider the fix complete.
5. Repeat starting from step 1. Do diagnosis, mitigation, and verification until all user traffic is healthy across all relevant paths you can identify in the system, and the system internals also appear healthy.

Additional guidelines:
- Use direct `kubectl` and `bash` investigation. Repository-local scripts are optional, not expected.
- If a tiny helper command or script would materially speed up repeated verification, you may use it, but do not spend incident time operationalizing the repository unless it is clearly necessary for the actual fix.
- Focus on real errors that cause user-visible impact. The application may not follow best practices, but do not get distracted by every minor deviation from ideal patterns. Instead, focus on diagnosing and fixing the issues that are actually causing the observed faults.
- Assume any command output or initial hypothesis may be incomplete or partially misleading. Revise your understanding when the live cluster proves it wrong.
- Do not hard-code detection or remediation around benchmark-specific guesses. Rely on live evidence from the cluster and source tree.
- Keep the investigation moving. Prioritize mitigation once you have enough evidence to act safely.

